#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2a82101-c28b-5830-81af-b93c42572664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341339} {:answer/id #uuid "023a78d9-3652-5259-a852-292d93e71fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341340} {:answer/id #uuid "b70c91ed-7e50-5684-aa1a-ce4f0f37fc33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341341} {:answer/id #uuid "57efe768-472c-5f2c-8dba-cab3a5ca79b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341342} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc584ed-5d2b-5c0f-a536-3a70bc8a2bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b70c91ed-7e50-5684-aa1a-ce4f0f37fc33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341341} :answer-field/id #uuid "f283d133-f208-5ecf-94cb-b68e3328340c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "02c2afcf-c82c-531e-a25b-5a0c4c7e8242" :question/math-academy-id "q-41811" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)=\\langle x^3+2y^2,x^2+\\ln y\\rangle$ for $y>0$, and let $D$ be the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$ and $(1,1)$. For $0<\\epsilon<1$, let $C_\\epsilon$ be the positively oriented (counterclockwise) boundary of $D_\\epsilon=D\\cap\\{y\\ge\\epsilon\\}$. Use Green's theorem on $D_\\epsilon$ to evaluate the limiting circulation\n\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lim}}=\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\oint_{C_\\epsilon}F\\cdot dr.$$\n\nThis explicit one-sided limit is the intended interpretation. The original ordinary line integral over the untruncated triangle is undefined because $\\ln y$ is undefined on its entire bottom edge." :question/worked-solution "For each $0<\\epsilon<1$, the field is continuously differentiable on an open neighborhood of $D_\\epsilon$, since that region stays in $y>0$. Its positively oriented boundary has vertices $(\\epsilon,\\epsilon)$, $(2-\\epsilon,\\epsilon)$ and $(1,1)$. Green's theorem therefore applies there.\n\nWith $P=x^3+2y^2$ and $Q=x^2+\\ln y$,\n\n$$Q_x-P_y=2x-4y.$$\n\nThe truncated triangle is described by $\\epsilon\\le y\\le1$ and $y\\le x\\le2-y$. Hence\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{C_\\epsilon}F\\cdot dr&=\\int_\\epsilon^1\\int_y^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,dx\\,dy\\\\&=\\int_\\epsilon^1(4-12y+8y^2)\\,dy\\\\&=\\frac23-4\\epsilon+6\\epsilon^2-\\frac83\\epsilon^3.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTaking $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ gives $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lim}}=\\frac23$, the unchanged displayed option b.\n\nThe boundary interpretation also converges directly. The $\\ln y\\,dy$ term has primitive $H(y)=y\\ln y-y$, and $H(y)\\to0$ as $y\\downarrow0$. Each sloping side thus has its own finite one-sided integral; no cancellation of divergent integrals or principal-value convention is needed. On each truncated horizontal bottom edge, $dy=0$, while $F$ is defined because $y=\\epsilon>0$. The polynomial terms also converge. These facts establish the specified limiting circulation without assigning a value to $F$ on the original edge $y=0$ or claiming ordinary Green's theorem applies to the untruncated boundary."}]}
